# The interface between Sokar and anything with a user in front of it.
#
# This file is the contract, not a description of one: the daemon serves it verbatim through
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription, and a test fails the build when a method is
# registered without appearing here or appears here without being registered. A client may
# therefore read it off a running daemon and trust that it matches.
#
# Everything is reached over one unix socket, owner-only, in Sokar's runtime directory
# ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sokar/sokard.sock). There is no port and no authentication of its own: the
# filesystem decides who may connect, and a remote client forwards the socket over SSH rather
# than reaching a listener.
#
# Framing is varlink's own: one JSON object per call, NUL-separated, no length prefix. A call
# carrying "more": true asks for a stream, and every reply but the last carries
# "continues": true.
#
# A field declared ?type is genuinely absent, not empty. Everything else is always present -
# a string that has no value arrives as "", because an interface has to render something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- compatibility
#
# A fleet is not upgraded at once, so a frontend has to work against a daemon older than
# itself. Three things make that possible, and a client should use all three.
#
# 1. The trailing 1 in the interface name is the promise. Within it this interface only ever
#    grows: new methods, new ?parameters, new reply fields. Nothing that is here will be
#    removed, renamed, given a different type, or made to mean something else. A change that
#    cannot be made that way becomes org.fuin.sokar.Tasks2, and a daemon serves both for at
#    least one release - so a client reads org.varlink.service.GetInfo, takes the highest
#    interface name it understands from the interfaces list, and uses that. Version numbers
#    are not compared to decide this.
#
# 2. Per feature, not per connection. Calling a method an older daemon does not have answers
#    org.varlink.service.MethodNotFound. That is a normal answer, not a fault: an interface
#    disables that one feature and keeps everything else. The same goes for a ?parameter an
#    older daemon ignores - it will behave as though it were not given, which is what its
#    default says it does.
#
# 3. Unknown values must not be fatal. New reply fields will appear in replies a client did
#    not expect, and new values will appear in enumerations it does not know - Outcome in
#    particular will gain entries. A client MUST ignore fields it does not recognize and MUST
#    render an unrecognized enumeration value rather than failing on it. This is part of the
#    contract: adding a value is not treated as a breaking change, so a client that cannot
#    survive one will break on a routine release.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInfo also reports the daemon's own version, which is a build
# version and moves independently of the number in the interface name. Show it, log it, and
# put it in a bug report - but do not gate features on it. What a daemon can do is answered
# by 1 and 2 above, which stay true no matter how the product is numbered.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription returns this file, from the running daemon.
# A client that wants certainty rather than assumption can read the contract off the thing
# it is talking to.

interface org.fuin.sokar.Tasks1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- types

# What the work in a task is doing, as against what its container is doing.
#
# "running" answers the container's question. This answers the one an operator has, and they are
# not the same: a task blocked on a question nobody saw and a task grinding through a build are
# both running: true, and that difference is the one that costs an afternoon.
type Activity (
  # Its container is not up. Stopped, finished or dead - all three are gone.
  DEAD,
  # Waiting for a person to answer something, said by the thing that asked rather than guessed
  # from how long it has been quiet. waitingFor says what about.
  WAITING,
  # Producing output.
  WORKING,
  # Up, producing nothing, and not waiting for anybody as far as anything can tell.
  IDLE,
  # Up, and nothing on this side can see what it is doing - a task somebody attached a terminal
  # to writes its work to that terminal. Its own value rather than "idle", because a state that
  # is silently wrong is worse than one that says it does not know.
  UNKNOWN
)

# How a person is meant to be involved in a task.
type Mode (
  # A terminal in the container, driven by hand.
  SHELL,
  # The agent's own session, attached, with a person working through it.
  AGENT,
  # Started with a prompt and left to run. Nobody is expected to be watching.
  UNATTENDED
)

# One task on this machine, running or not.
#
# A task is a container plus the helpers Sokar started beside it. Both halves matter: a
# container that is up with no helpers has lost its gate or its clearance watcher, and is not
# the same thing as a healthy task.
type Task (
  # Container name, which is what every other method takes as its task parameter.
  name: string,
  # Project the task belongs to, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  project: string,
  # offline, guarded or online - or "" when nothing recorded it.
  securityClass: string,
  # The container runtime's own words, such as "Up 4 minutes" or "Exited (143) 2 seconds ago".
  # Free text meant for a person; do not parse it. Use running for the decision.
  state: string,
  # Whether the runtime says it is up.
  running: bool,
  # How many recorded helper processes are alive.
  helpers: int,
  # Agent running in it, by the name Agents reports. "" when it has none, or when the task was
  # started by a Sokar that did not record it.
  agent: string,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved: SHELL, AGENT or UNATTENDED, spelled as Mode spells
  # them. A string rather than the Mode type because it has a fourth state the type does not: ""
  # for a task started before this field existed, where nothing recorded one and inventing an
  # answer would be worse than admitting there is none.
  mode: string,
  # What an unattended task was asked to do. Kept after it has finished, because "what was this
  # asked to do" is a question people have about a task that is over. "" for the other modes.
  prompt: string,
  # Ref its work goes to, such as refs/sokar/incoming/shell. "" when it has no gate.
  branch: string,
  # When the current state began, ISO-8601: a running task since it started, a stopped one since
  # it exited. "" when the runtime could not say - a container that was created and never started
  # has no honest answer. This is what "idle for forty minutes" is arithmetic on; state is not.
  since: string,
  # What the work is doing, beside state rather than instead of it.
  activity: Activity,
  # What it is waiting to be told, when activity is WAITING. "" otherwise.
  waitingFor: string,
  # What this task does with a blocked connection: "prompt", "allow", "deny" or "off" - the value
  # Start was given. "" when nothing recorded it, which is every task started before this field
  # existed.
  #
  # "off" is the one to mark wherever the task appears: nothing asks and nothing is refused, so
  # the firewall is loaded and no decision is ever put to anybody. It is a state somebody chose,
  # and an interface that shows it like any other run is hiding the choice.
  clearance: string,
  # A changeable caption somebody gave this task. "" when nobody has, which is every task by
  # default and is a normal state rather than a gap.
  #
  # BESIDE the name, never instead of it: 'name' is what every other method takes and what
  # somebody types into sokar at the machine, so a row that showed only this would make the
  # interface and the command line disagree about what a thing is called. Absent and empty are
  # deliberately the same state.
  label: string,
  # Whether this task's own work is waiting at the gate. 1 when its ref is waiting for review, 0
  # otherwise - and 0 for an online project, where nothing is ever reviewed, which is not a
  # smaller number but a question the class does not have.
  #
  # Answered here rather than left to a join, because there is no join to make: 'name' is a
  # CONTAINER name (sokar-<project>-<task>-<run>), PendingPush.name is a TASK name, and several
  # containers over time share one ref. Anything lined up from the two would be right for at most
  # one of them and silently wrong for the rest.
  waiting: int
)

# What became of a Stop or a Resume.
#
# One enumeration serves both because one enumeration implements both. A client should render
# every value it can receive: a refusal is the interesting outcome, not an error case.
type Outcome (
  # Stop: the name is not one Sokar created. Nothing was touched.
  NOT_A_TASK,
  # Stop: no container and no helpers by that name.
  NOTHING_TO_STOP,
  # Stop: the container is down and the helpers are gone.
  STOPPED,
  # Stop: refused, because the task holds commits that never reached the gate. work says what.
  # Re-ask with purge to discard it, or with rescue to push it into the mirror first.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Stop: nothing could say whether there was work, so it was not removed.
  NOTHING_KNOWS,
  # Stop: rescue was asked for, but the container is already down and cannot be read.
  RESCUE_NEEDS_IT_RUNNING,
  # Stop: rescue was attempted and failed. detail says why. Nothing was removed.
  RESCUE_FAILED,
  # Resume: the container is up again and the helpers were restarted.
  RESUMED,
  # Resume: it was already running. Nothing was started.
  ALREADY_RUNNING,
  # Resume: there is no container by that name to start.
  NO_CONTAINER,
  # Resume: the container started, but nothing recorded which helpers it should have.
  NO_HELPERS_RECORDED
)

# An agent binary installed on this machine.
type Agent (
  # Name the binary calls itself by, which is what Start takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name.
  label: string,
  # The command it runs inside the container.
  binary: string,
  # The build this agent pins, from its own manifest, or "" when it pins none. NOT what the
  # binary answers if asked its version: nothing here executes it to find out, so two machines
  # showing the same value are running the same declared build.
  version: string,
  # Where the binary was found.
  from: string,
  # Hosts this agent needs, added to a task's egress on top of the project's own.
  allowedDomains: []string,
  # Hosts this agent declares and is deliberately NOT given. Not the same as absent from
  # allowedDomains: these were asked for and refused on purpose, which is worth showing beside
  # what was granted rather than leaving as a silence.
  refusedDomains: []string,
  # What this agent fetches when its image is built, each with the digest that pins it. Empty is
  # a real state and not a gap: an agent that writes its tool into the image fetches nothing, so
  # it has a pinned version and no digest at all.
  artifacts: []InstallArtifact,
  # The identity this agent's commits carry, from its own manifest. This is what tells a person
  # looking at a commit whether an agent or a person wrote it, and it is what the pre-push guard
  # on an operator's own checkout matches on.
  commitsAs: GitIdentity
)

# How an agent signs its work.
type GitIdentity (
  # Author name on its commits.
  name: string,
  # Author address on its commits.
  email: string
)

# One file an agent fetches while its image is built.
type InstallArtifact (
  # Where it is fetched from.
  url: string,
  # Expected SHA-256, or "" when there is none - in which case 'unverified' says whether that is
  # deliberate.
  sha256: string,
  # Absolute path it is installed at.
  target: string,
  # Whether it is knowingly unverifiable. An artifact with no digest and no reason is a mistake;
  # one with a reason is a decision somebody made and can be shown as such.
  unverified: bool,
  # Why it cannot be verified. "" unless 'unverified'.
  reason: string
)

# An installed agent binary that never runs: either an earlier location holds one of the same
# file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first.
type ShadowedAgent (
  # The binary that is installed and not in use.
  path: string,
  # The one that runs instead. Locations are searched most specific first - the operator's own
  # directory, then the packaged one - so this is a rule and not an accident of scan order.
  usedInstead: string
)

# One entry in the vault. Never its value: this interface cannot be used to read a secret.
type Credential (
  name: string,
  # What kind of credential it is, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  type: string,
  # Length of the stored value, so an interface can show that something is there.
  characters: int
)

# A push waiting at the gate for a decision.
type PendingPush (
  # Ref name under refs/sokar/incoming/, which is what Review, Approve and Reject take.
  name: string,
  commit: string,
  # First line of the commit message.
  subject: string,
  # How long it has been waiting, as words: "4 minutes".
  waiting: string,
  # When it arrived, ISO-8601.
  at: string
)

# A curated group of hosts a project can opt into by name.
type EgressSet (
  # What a project writes in its egress.sets list, and what SetEgress takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name, for a listing.
  label: string,
  # The hosts it grants, in the order the file declares them.
  domains: []string
)

# A host a task may reach, and who granted it.
type EgressHost (
  host: string,
  # "agent <name>", "provider <name>", "set <name>", "project" or "upstream". The first grant
  # wins, so a host in both a set and an agent's list is reported as the agent's.
  origin: string
)

# How far a change to a running task goes. There is no default: "this run only" and "this run
# and the project file" are different intentions, and a client that did not say which it meant
# must not have one picked for it.
type Scope (
  # The running task, and nothing else. The project file is untouched and the next task will
  # ask again - a resumed task loses it too, because its ruleset and resolver are rebuilt from
  # what is on disk.
  RUN,
  # The running task, and the project file, so the next task starts with it.
  RUN_AND_PROJECT
)

# What became of widening a running task.
type WidenOutcome (
  # Applied to the running task. persisted says whether the file was written too.
  WIDENED,
  # NarrowTask: taken back from the running task.
  NARROWED,
  # What it would do, having changed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Every name was already granted to this run.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # No such task, or it is not up. Nothing can be changed in a container that is gone.
  NOT_RUNNING,
  # The project is offline, so its tasks reach nothing and this is not a way around that.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # The run WAS widened and the project file was not, because nothing knows where it is. Not a
  # failure: the task can now reach the host, and detail says why the file was not written.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # The run could not be widened at all. detail says why.
  FAILED
)

# What became of a change to a project's egress.
type EgressOutcome (
  # Applied and written to the project file.
  CHANGED,
  # What it would do. Nothing was written, because dryRun was set.
  PREVIEWED,
  # The file already said that. Nothing was written.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # A named set is not installed on this machine, so the file would name nothing real. Nothing
  # was written; detail says which.
  NO_SUCH_SET,
  # The project's security class forbids declaring egress at all - an offline project declares
  # nothing. detail says so in the words the project reader uses.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # The project file could not be read. detail says why.
  UNREADABLE,
  # The change was right and the file could not be written. detail says why; opens and closes
  # still describe what it would have done.
  NOT_WRITTEN
)

# A project this machine knows about.
#
# Not something Sokar creates: a project is somebody's directory with a project.yml in it, and
# this reports what has been seen of one rather than owning it.
type Project (
  # Project name, as its file declares it.
  name: string,
  # offline, guarded or online. "" when no task has recorded one.
  securityClass: string,
  # Absolute path of its project.yml on the daemon's machine, which every gate method and Start
  # take. "" when nothing recorded one, or when the recorded file is no longer there - reported
  # absent rather than as a path nothing can read, so a call made with it cannot fail for a
  # reason that looks like a fault in the daemon.
  file: string,
  # The gate's mirror for it. "" when it has never used the gate.
  mirror: string,
  # How many pushes are waiting for review in that mirror.
  pending: int,
  # How many of its tasks exist right now, running or stopped.
  tasks: int,
  # How many of those are up. A project with none is the ordinary case - between tasks, or after
  # one was stopped and can still be resumed - so both numbers are given rather than one being
  # inferred from the other. This list is every project, not only the busy ones.
  running: int,
  # Whether a task image for this project exists, so work can start here without building one
  # first. That is the whole claim: it does NOT say the image still matches the project file as
  # it stands now. A project that has never run answers false, which is a normal state and not a
  # fault - it means the first task here will spend minutes building before anything happens.
  prepared: bool,
  # ABSENT, READY, STALE or UNKNOWN - what 'prepared' cannot say.
  #
  # STALE means an image exists and was built before the project file changed under it, so work
  # would start in something the file no longer describes and nothing would mention it. That is
  # the state this field exists for; a bool could only ever say whether something was there.
  #
  # UNKNOWN is an image that does not record what it was built from - built by an older Sokar, or
  # belonging to a project whose file has moved. Not stale: nothing here knows either way, and
  # reporting it as stale would send somebody rebuilding for no reason.
  #
  # Only the base image and the image snippet decide this. Egress, limits and the upstream change
  # what a task may do rather than what it is built from, and counting them would call an image
  # stale after an edit that could not have changed it - which teaches people to ignore the word.
  preparedState: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this project's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # behindReason is MEASURED - it is 0 for every other reason, and 0 there does not mean up to
  # date. Never render this number without behindMeasured beside it.
  behind: int,
  # When 'behind' was measured, RFC 3339. "" when it never was.
  #
  # Not optional decoration. The daemon measures on a timer, because a reply field that fetched
  # would make this list occasionally take thirty seconds for reasons nothing on screen explains
  # - so what you get is a number with an age, and the only thing worse than a stale answer is a
  # stale answer that looks fresh.
  behindMeasured: string,
  # Why 'behind' is what it is. New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than
  # throwing, by the same rule as every other enumeration here.
  #
  #   MEASURED       the number is real, and behindMeasured says when it was taken
  #   NEVER_CHECKED  nothing has looked yet. NOT the same as being up to date
  #   NO_UPSTREAM    the mirror was created empty, so there is nothing to be behind
  #   OFFLINE        the project reaches nothing by class, so asking would break that promise
  #   FAILED         looking failed; behindDetail says what git said
  behindReason: string,
  # What went wrong, for FAILED. "" otherwise. Prose for a person - never parsed, and never
  # rendered as a number.
  behindDetail: string
)

# One log a task has written.
type Log (
  # Pass this to Tail unchanged.
  name: string,
  # How large it is now.
  bytes: int,
  # When it was last written, ISO-8601.
  at: string
)

# A connection a task tried to make and was stopped for.
#
# This is the one event with a deadline. The task is blocked while it waits, and the watcher
# gives up after its own timeout - so an interface that shows these must show them as they
# arrive, which is why Prompts is a stream and not a poll.
#
# The answer comes back on the same stream: the same destination again, with verdict set. An
# interface should match it to the question by task and key rather than showing it twice.
type Prompt (
  # Container name, for routing the answer back to the right task.
  task: string,
  # Pass this to Decide unchanged. It is derived from the other fields, but the derivation is
  # the daemon's and a client that rebuilt it would answer a prompt that does not exist.
  key: string,
  # Host or address the task asked for.
  destination: string,
  # tcp or udp.
  protocol: string,
  port: int,
  # When it was blocked, ISO-8601 - and on an event carrying a verdict, when it was decided.
  # The two are the same field because they are the same event twice; keep the question's own
  # value if you want the block time of an answered prompt.
  at: string,
  # Where in the ruleset it was stopped, for an operator who needs to know which rule fired.
  # Empty on an event carrying a verdict: nothing decided it a second time.
  prefix: string,
  # Absent while the question is open. Once it is settled the same destination arrives again
  # carrying "allow", "deny" or "timeout", and that is the only way an interface learns that a
  # prompt ran out: nothing will ask about it again, so a question that simply stops arriving is
  # indistinguishable from one still waiting for its operator.
  verdict: ?string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- what exists

# Every task on this machine, running or stopped.
method List() -> (tasks: []Task)

# The same answer, again whenever it changes.
#
# Called without "more" this answers once, exactly as List does, so a client that cannot
# stream is not locked out of the data.
#
# Called with "more" it sends the whole list on every change and nothing in between. The age
# in state is deliberately not a change: it moves on its own, and a view that redrew for it
# would redraw every second forever.
method Watch() -> (tasks: []Task)

# Agent binaries installed on this machine.
method Agents() -> (
  # One entry per agent NAME, never two: they are held by name, so a name appears once whatever
  # is installed. A second binary claiming a name already taken does not appear here at all - it
  # lands in 'failures'.
  agents: []Agent,
  # Binaries that are installed and could not be asked what they are, by file name. An agent
  # that fails to describe itself is unusable, and leaving it out would read as not installed.
  # A name collision is NOT here: nothing is broken in that case, one copy runs.
  failures: [string]string,
  # Binaries that are installed and never run. Two causes, one consequence: an earlier location
  # holds the same file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first. Neither has
  # an Agent entry to carry a flag, which is why this is a list rather than a field on one.
  shadowed: []ShadowedAgent
)

# What the vault holds, without any of it.
method Credentials() -> (
  # Where the vault file is.
  vault: string,
  # Whether that file exists.
  exists: bool,
  credentials: []Credential,
  # Whether the list can be believed. The vault opens only if its passphrase is already in the
  # kernel keyring - a daemon has no terminal to ask at - so an empty list from a locked vault
  # and an empty list from an empty vault are different things an interface must not conflate.
  # False means locked or undecryptable, and only then is there a passphrase to ask for. A vault
  # that does not exist yet is readable and empty: there is nothing to unlock.
  readable: bool
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- running tasks

# Starts a task.
#
# The long one. With "more" it streams the build as it happens, one reply per line, and an
# interface should show them: building an image takes minutes, and showing nothing for that
# long is indistinguishable from having hung.
#
# The task outlives this call and this connection. Closing the socket mid-build does not
# cancel it; List shows it afterwards.
method Start(
  # Task name within the project. Default: "shell".
  task: ?string,
  # Path to the project file, as the CLI takes it. Default: "project.yml".
  project: ?string,
  # Agent to run, by the name Agents reports. Default: the only one installed, or an error naming
  # them all when several are.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Default: the agent's own declared default, or an error when it
  # declares none. A project file names neither an agent nor a provider.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this run.
  credentialType: ?string,
  # Lifetime of the gate token. Default: 8.
  tokenHours: ?int,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Runs with no workspace and no git gate: the agent gets an empty directory and can commit
  # nowhere.
  noGate: ?bool,
  # Does everything up to starting the container, then stops and reports. Nothing is created.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # What to do with a blocked connection: prompt, allow, deny or off. Default: "prompt".
  clearance: ?string,
  # Keeps the container after it exits.
  keep: ?bool,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved, recorded on the task and answered by List and Watch.
  # Default: UNATTENDED when a prompt is given, SHELL otherwise - so a caller that gives a prompt
  # need not repeat itself, and one that gives neither gets the honest answer for a container
  # nobody is attached to.
  mode: ?Mode,
  # What an unattended task is asked to do. Recorded on the task and kept after it finishes.
  #
  # Giving one makes this call RUN the agent, not merely start the container: the call lasts as
  # long as the run does, and with "more" the agent's own output arrives as it is produced. The
  # exit code in the reply is then the agent's. Without a prompt the container comes up and the
  # call returns, as it always did.
  prompt: ?string,
  # Model to ask the agent for, when it declares a model flag. Ignored otherwise.
  model: ?string,
  # Turn limit, when the agent declares one.
  maxTurns: ?int,
  # How long the agent may run, in minutes. Default: 30. On expiry the run is killed and whatever
  # it produced is kept - the exit code is 124 and the log holds what it managed to say.
  minutes: ?int
) -> (
  # One line of output. Set on every reply while continues is true, and only then.
  line: ?string,
  # Container name of the started task. Set on the final reply, and only then.
  container: ?string,
  # What the launch returned. 0 is success. Set on the final reply.
  exitCode: ?int,
  # Everything that was printed, for a caller that did not ask to stream. Empty when it did.
  output: ?[]string
)

# Stops a task and removes what is left of it.
#
# Refuses rather than destroys. A task holding commits that never reached the gate comes back
# as HOLDS_WORK and is left exactly as it was; the caller decides whether that work is worth
# keeping and asks again.
method Stop(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # Discard unpushed work and remove it anyway.
  purge: ?bool,
  # Push what it holds into the gate's mirror first, then remove it.
  rescue: ?bool,
  # Remove it even when nothing can say what it holds.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # What it holds, when that is why it was refused. "" otherwise.
  work: string,
  # Where rescued work was put, when it was rescued. "" otherwise.
  rescuedRef: string,
  # Whether the container was removed.
  removed: bool,
  # How many helper processes were stopped.
  helpers: int,
  # How many helper processes are still alive afterwards. Not zero is worth showing.
  surviving: int,
  # Why, in words, for an outcome that needs one. "" otherwise.
  detail: string,
  # How many paths the container had that its image did not - what the agent installed inside it,
  # which is destroyed with the container and has nowhere to arrive, unlike the workspace. Counted
  # only when it was removed, and zero otherwise. Show it beside the removal: nothing else records
  # that any of it existed.
  discarded: int
)

# Starts a stopped task's container again, with the helpers it is recorded as having had.
method Resume(task: string) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # How many helpers were started.
  started: int,
  # How many the task is recorded as having had. Fewer started than recorded is a partial
  # resume, and an interface should say so.
  recorded: int,
  # Set when the image the container was built from has changed since. "" otherwise.
  imageDrift: string,
  # What went wrong per helper, for the ones that did not come back.
  problems: []string
)

# One task a panic stopped, or would have stopped.
type PanickedTask (
  # Container name, which is what Resume takes.
  name: string,
  # How many helper processes it had.
  helpers: int,
  # Helpers that outlived the stop and have to be killed by hand. Empty is the normal case.
  surviving: []string
)

# Stops every running task and every helper any of them started.
#
# No task names, on purpose: somebody reaching for this is not in a position to list what is
# running first, and a stop that needs an inventory is one they will get wrong.
#
# IT STOPS; IT NEVER REMOVES. Every workspace, log and unpushed commit survives, and Resume brings
# a task back with the work it had. An interface that presented this as a cleanup would send
# somebody looking for work that is still exactly where it was. Discarding is a separate decision,
# made afterwards, by name, with Stop(purge:).
method Panic(
  # Lists what would be stopped and stops nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # Every task that was running, in the order they were stopped. Empty means nothing was running.
  tasks: []PanickedTask,
  # Every helper that outlived its stop, across all tasks. Non-empty means somebody has processes
  # to kill by hand, and they are named rather than counted.
  surviving: []string,
  # Whether this only listed what would happen.
  previewed: bool
)

# Drops the cached vault passphrase, so the next thing that needs it has to ask.
#
# The only call that changes the credential store. There is deliberately no Unlock: a daemon has
# no terminal to take a passphrase at, so it can shut the vault and can never open it - unlocking
# stays at the machine, with 'sokar vault unlock'.
method Lock() -> (
  # Whether the kernel keyring exists here at all. Without it nothing can be cached, so nothing
  # ever was, and "locked" would claim a change that did not happen.
  keyring: bool,
  # Whether a passphrase was cached and has now been dropped. False means it was already locked -
  # which is the wanted state either way, so this is not a failure.
  wasCached: bool,
  # Running tasks whose credential proxy already read the credential and holds it in its own
  # memory, where locking cannot reach. Stopping them is what ends that. An interface reporting
  # "locked" without this would be claiming more than happened.
  holding: int
)

# Why work could not start here, or that it could.
#
# New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than throwing, by the same rule as
# every other enumeration here. The three that mean different ACTIONS are NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN
# (pick a provider), CREDENTIAL_MISSING (store a secret) and VAULT_LOCKED (unlock it) - showing
# any of those as another is telling somebody to do the wrong thing.
type StartOutcome (
  # Nothing is in the way.
  READY,
  # The project file is not there, or cannot be read.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # Nothing is installed to run. A task with no agent can still be started as a plain shell, so
  # this is not "the machine is broken".
  NO_AGENT,
  # A name was given and nothing is installed under it.
  UNKNOWN_AGENT,
  # Several are installed and none was named. Somebody chooses an agent.
  SEVERAL_AGENTS,
  # The agent names no default provider and none was chosen. Somebody chooses a PROVIDER - not a
  # secret, which is why this is not folded into CREDENTIAL_MISSING.
  NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN,
  # The provider asked for is not declared on this machine.
  UNKNOWN_PROVIDER,
  # The provider is declared but does not serve the dialect this agent speaks.
  WRONG_DIALECT,
  # The vault cannot be read, so this question cannot be answered yet. NOT a missing credential:
  # the one thing that helps is unlocking, and telling somebody to store a secret they already
  # have is the instruction that cannot help them.
  VAULT_LOCKED,
  # The vault is readable and holds nothing under the name this run would look for. 'credential'
  # names it.
  CREDENTIAL_MISSING,
  # Something is stored and cannot be brokered - the wrong kind for this provider. 'detail' says
  # what.
  CREDENTIAL_UNUSABLE
)

# Whether work could start here, asked before anything is created.
#
# This exists because the only other way to find out was to start a task and read an exit code and
# a stream of prose. It takes the same choices Start takes and answers from the same code, so a
# check that says READY and a Start that refuses cannot disagree.
#
# What it CANNOT tell you: whether the credential is still valid at the provider. It answers that
# something usable is stored under the name this run would look for, which is a different claim.
method CanStart(
  # Path to the project file, as Start takes it. Omitted, no project file is checked.
  project: ?string,
  # Agent to run. Omitted, the only one installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Omitted, the agent's own declared default.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this question, as Start takes it.
  credentialType: ?string
) -> (
  # Whether work could start. True exactly when outcome is READY, so branch on whichever suits -
  # but never infer the ACTION from this alone: three different things somebody has to do all
  # arrive as false.
  ready: bool,
  outcome: StartOutcome,
  # The agent this was answered for. "" when none was settled.
  agent: string,
  # The provider serving it. "" when the agent is not brokered, or none was settled.
  provider: string,
  # The vault key this run would look for. "" when the question never got that far.
  #
  # The name ACTUALLY looked for, not the one that ought to apply: a vault written before
  # credentials were keyed by provider still answers under the agent's own name, so this can be
  # either. Render this, not a name of your own composing - otherwise the sentence on screen names
  # a key that would be right in a vault the person does not have.
  credential: string,
  # Prose for a person, always set. Never parsed.
  detail: string
)

# Gives a task a changeable caption, or removes one.
#
# NOT a rename. A task's name is its identity in four places - the container, the gate ref it
# pushes to, the workspace and the log files - and moving a ref that may have unreviewed pushes
# behind it is a different and much larger thing. This changes what a person reads in a list and
# nothing else.
method Label(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # The caption. Omitted or blank removes it.
  label: ?string
) -> (
  #   LABELLED      the caption was written down
  #   CLEARED       the caption was removed
  #   NOT_A_TASK    no such task, or its state directory is gone
  #   NOT_RECORDED  it exists but recorded nothing about itself - started by an older Sokar
  #   FAILED        the profile could not be written
  outcome: string,
  # What the task's caption is now. "" after CLEARED, and "" for every outcome that changed
  # nothing.
  label: string
)

# Reads one of a task's log files, and with "more" follows it as it is written.
#
# The log name is checked against the files that are there rather than resolved as a path.
# The curated egress sets installed on this machine.
#
# What a chooser offers, so nobody has to author a host list. Scanned from a directory each time
# it is asked, so a set an operator dropped in appears without anything being rebuilt - and
# theirs wins over a packaged one of the same name, which is why the locations are given in the
# order they are searched.
#
# Not every set can be complete: os-packages-fedora reaches mirrors named by a mirrorlist, which
# differ by region and by day, so hosts beyond the list arrive as clearance prompts. An interface
# should not present it as equivalent to the others.
method Sets() -> (
  sets: []EgressSet,
  # Directories searched, most specific first. Empty means none is installed.
  locations: []string
)

# What a project may reach, and who granted each host.
#
# The agent's own hosts and its provider's are included, because what an operator wants to know
# is what a task will reach and half of it is not in the project file. Which agent that is
# follows the same rule a run does: the one named, or the only one installed.
method Egress(
  # Path of the project file, from Projects.file.
  project: string,
  # Agent whose grants to include. Default: the only one installed.
  agent: ?string
) -> (
  hosts: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts an agent declares it asks for and is deliberately not given. Distinguishable from a
  # host nobody added, which is the distinction the firewall itself cannot make.
  refused: []string
)

# Changes what a project may reach, or says what a change would do.
#
# The highest-consequence edit in the product, so it answers in hosts rather than in set names: a
# set is a name for several hosts and whoever adds one is entitled to see them. The project file
# is edited in place - comments, key order and quoting survive - and the result is parsed by the
# reader that would have to read it later before anything is written.
#
# Nothing here applies to a task that is already running: a container's ruleset and resolver are
# built when it starts.
method SetEgress(
  # Path of the project file, from Projects.file.
  project: string,
  # Curated sets to declare. A set this machine does not have is refused rather than written.
  addSets: ?[]string,
  removeSets: ?[]string,
  # Hosts to declare directly, for a mirror no set covers.
  addDomains: ?[]string,
  removeDomains: ?[]string,
  # Stop before writing and report what would have happened.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: EgressOutcome,
  # Hosts the change adds, with the origin that grants each. In the order they were granted.
  opens: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts it takes away.
  closes: []EgressHost,
  # What reaching a forge costs a guarded project, when this change opens one and the project
  # did not already reach one. "" otherwise - said once, when it becomes true.
  cost: string,
  # Why it was refused, or what could not be written. "" when nothing went wrong.
  detail: string
)

# Gives a task that is already running somewhere new to reach.
#
# The alternative is to stop the run and start another one, which throws away a workspace, a gate
# token, commits that never reached the gate and an agent halfway through something - to add one
# host. This changes it in place: the resolver is told without being restarted, and the name is
# recorded so the clearance watcher lets it through without asking anybody.
#
# Names only. A set is a name for several hosts and granting one would be the same operation
# repeated; narrowing a running task is not offered at all, because taking a grant away from a
# container that already has it is a different thing and is not decided yet.
#
# **The attempt that was already refused is not retried.** The packet was dropped; whether the
# agent tries again is the agent's business. An interface may say the host is now reachable and
# must not say that the request which just failed will now succeed.
# Takes names back from a running task, and optionally from its project file.
#
# THIS STOPS NEW CONNECTIONS AND NOT THE ONES ALREADY RUNNING. The name stops resolving and its
# recorded addresses come out of the firewall, so nothing new can be reached - but the ruleset
# accepts established traffic without consulting the set again, so a transfer in progress runs to
# its end. Do not render this as "the host is now unreachable": it is not, yet. If a transfer has
# to stop, stopping the task is what does that.
#
# The addresses removed are the ones recorded when each grant was applied, never the answer to
# resolving the name again now - a CDN or round-robin answers the daemon and the container
# differently, and the addresses that differ are exactly the ones that would be left open.
#
# 'scope' is required, exactly as for WidenTask. Narrowing only the run when somebody meant the
# project too leaves the next task starting with the host still open.
# Turns enforcement on or off on a task that is already running.
#
# Deliberately not WidenTask with a special value. Those grant and withdraw names, and that the
# firewall stays loaded is what they mean; folding "stop asking about anything" into them would
# make one method mean two unrelated things, and the quiet one would be the dangerous one.
#
# What it cannot undo: turning enforcement off does not recall a connection that was already
# refused - the packet was dropped and nothing retries it - and turning it back on does not recall
# anything waved through while it was off. The ruleset is loaded throughout, so this never opens a
# destination by itself; what changes is whether a blocked connection produces a question.
method SetClearance(
  task: string,
  # prompt, allow, deny or off.
  mode: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # CHANGED, UNCHANGED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_TASK, NOT_RUNNING, UNKNOWN_MODE, NOT_RECORDED or
  # FAILED.
  #
  # UNCHANGED is not a failure: it was already in that mode and nothing was restarted, which is
  # worth saying rather than reporting a change that did not happen.
  outcome: string,
  # The mode before. "" for a task started before this was recorded.
  was: string,
  # The mode after. "" when nothing changed.
  now: string,
  detail: string
)

method NarrowTask(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # Names to take back. Anything not currently granted to this run is ignored, not an error.
  domains: []string,
  # RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT. Required; there is no default.
  scope: Scope,
  # Reports what it would take back, having changed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  #   NARROWED         taken back from the run; persisted says whether the file was too
  #   PREVIEWED        what it would take back, having changed nothing
  #   NO_CHANGE        none of these names is granted to this run
  #   NOT_RUNNING      no such task, or it is not up
  #   NO_PROJECT_FILE  the RUN was narrowed and the file was not; NOT a failure
  #   FAILED           the run could not be narrowed; detail says why
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # Names taken back, in the order they were asked for.
  closes: []string,
  # How many addresses came out of the firewall. Zero with a non-empty 'closes' is a real state:
  # the name was granted and the container never reached it, so nothing was in the set.
  addresses: int,
  # Whether the project file was changed as well.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused or what went wrong. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

method WidenTask(
  # Container name, from Task.name.
  task: string,
  # Host names to grant. A grant covers what is under it, as the resolver does.
  domains: []string,
  # Required. RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT.
  scope: Scope,
  # Stop before changing anything and report what would happen.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # The names it grants, in the order they were asked for.
  opens: []string,
  # Whether the project file was written as well. Always false for RUN.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused, or what could not be written. "" when nothing went wrong.
  detail: string
)

# Every project this machine knows about, alphabetically.
#
# The list is assembled from what is durable: the gate mirrors, the tasks that exist, and the
# project files task starts have recorded. A project appears here once somebody has run a task
# with it - there is no registration step, because nobody would run one.
#
# This is the method that makes the gate reachable from an interface. Pending, Review, Approve,
# Reject and Start all take a project file path, and a client on another machine has no
# filesystem here to find one in.
# One thing a project deletion removes.
type Removal (
  # MIRROR, IMAGE, BUILD, REGISTRY, UPSTREAM_RECORD or TASK.
  kind: string,
  # The thing itself - a path, an image name, a container name - as a person would recognise it.
  what: string
)

type DeleteOutcome (
  # Everything Sokar built for it is gone.
  DELETED,
  # What would go, having removed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Refused: work reached the gate and nobody reviewed it. The same name Stop uses, for the same
  # situation, so it can read the same on a screen.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Refused: tasks are still up.
  TASKS_RUNNING,
  # Nothing here knows that project.
  NO_SUCH_PROJECT,
  # Something could not be removed. detail says what.
  FAILED
)

# Removes what Sokar built for a project.
#
# NOT "deleting the project". The project file is the operator's, in their own directory, and so
# are the checkout and the real upstream - none is touched, and 'keeps' names them so a
# confirmation can say so without the interface having to know which things are Sokar's. What goes
# is the mirror, the image, the build directory, the registry entry, the recorded upstream
# distance, and every task with its container, state and logs.
#
# Afterwards the project file is still there and running a task in that directory builds all of it
# again. That is the sentence somebody needs before they press it, more than they need the list.
#
# It REFUSES rather than decides. Unreviewed pushes exist only in the mirror - nothing else has
# them anywhere - and a running task is work cut off mid-flight. 'force' is how somebody says they
# meant it.
method DeleteProject(
  # Project name, as Projects reports it.
  project: string,
  # Reports what would go, having removed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Proceeds despite unreviewed work or running tasks.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: DeleteOutcome,
  # What goes, or went. Also filled for a refusal, so a confirmation can show the cost beside the
  # reason it was stopped.
  removes: []Removal,
  # What is deliberately NOT touched, named.
  keeps: []string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed. Non-empty with HOLDS_WORK, and also under force - it is what force
  # destroys.
  unreviewed: []string,
  # Tasks that are up. Non-empty with TASKS_RUNNING, and also under force.
  running: []string,
  # Why it was refused or what went wrong. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

method Projects() -> (projects: []Project)

# What this node calls itself.
#
# GetInfo answers a vendor and a version, which says what a daemon is and never which one. This
# answers which one - so a client can tell that two entries in a list of machines are the same
# node reached two ways, which it cannot work out for itself: a hostname has many spellings, and
# a socket somebody else forwarded looks nothing like your own tunnel to the same place.
#
# The failure it prevents is not cosmetic. The same node listed twice delivers every clearance
# question twice, and answering one leaves the other on screen until it expires.
# Creates a project file, having checked the answers against this machine first.
#
# The checking is what a client cannot do for itself: whether a security class is spelled right,
# whether an egress set exists on this machine, whether the name survives becoming an image tag
# and an nftables set name. An answer accepted here and rejected at the first task start is
# rejected far from where it was given.
method CreateProject(
  # Absolute path of the project.yml to write.
  file: string,
  name: string,
  # offline, guarded or online.
  securityClass: string,
  baseImage: string,
  # Required by 'online', which pushes to it directly. Ignored by the other classes, which reach
  # their upstream only through the gate.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Egress set names, checked against the sets this machine has.
  sets: []string,
  # Checks and renders, writing nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # CREATED, PREVIEWED, ALREADY_EXISTS, INVALID or FAILED.
  #
  # ALREADY_EXISTS is a refusal and never an overwrite: the file may be somebody's whole
  # configuration, and this is the one operation that would replace it with nothing to restore
  # from.
  outcome: string,
  file: string,
  # The file as it would be written, for review. Filled even on a refusal - seeing what was
  # rejected is most of understanding why.
  content: string,
  problems: []Problem,
  detail: string
)

# Something wrong with one answer.
type Problem (
  # Which answer, named as the call named it.
  field: string,
  what: string,
  # Whether it stops the project being created. A non-fatal one is worth showing and not worth
  # blocking on - a base image that is not on the machine yet will simply be pulled.
  fatal: bool
)

# Builds a project's task image without starting anything.
#
# The first task in a project otherwise waits minutes for something that has nothing to do with
# the work. Use with "more": a build takes minutes, and showing nothing for that long is
# indistinguishable from having hung.
method Prepare(
  # Absolute path of the project file, as Projects reports it.
  project: string,
  # Whose tooling to install. Omit when only one agent is installed. An image with no agent is a
  # real thing to want - it is what somebody working in the container by hand needs - so this is
  # not refused when no agent is found.
  agent: ?string,
  # CACHED, AGENT or EVERYTHING. Default CACHED.
  #
  # CACHED is not "skip the build": the build runs and podman's cache decides layer by layer, so
  # an edited project file rebuilds what it changed and nothing else. It is what every task start
  # already does.
  #
  # AGENT replaces the agent's tooling and keeps the base image and its packages. There is no
  # podman flag for "rebuild from here" - it works by changing a build argument placed where the
  # agent's layers begin, which invalidates the cache from that line down. So it is real, and it
  # is a mechanism rather than a switch.
  #
  # EVERYTHING discards the base image's packages too, which means downloading them again.
  rebuild: ?string,
  # Says what it would do and does nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # PREPARED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The image name, or "" when nothing was built.
  image: string,
  # The depth actually used, so an unrecognised one is visible rather than silently defaulted.
  rebuild: string,
  # Progress, when this was not called with "more". Empty when it was streamed.
  output: []string,
  # Why it failed, naming the step. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Measures one project's distance from its upstream, now.
#
# Its own operation on purpose. The distance is otherwise measured on a timer and read from a
# record, because measuring reaches the network - and a listing that fetched would make the queue
# cost what a listing must not. So this cannot be a flag on a read; it is something somebody asked
# for.
#
# It goes through the same measurement the timer uses and writes the same record, so a triggered
# fetch and a timed one cannot disagree about how far behind a project is, and Projects shows
# whichever ran last without being told.
method SyncUpstream(
  # Project NAME, as Projects reports it - not the path Prepare takes. A path answers as though
  # the project were unknown, which on the screens these feed is a legitimate-looking answer
  # rather than an error.
  project: string) -> (
  # MEASURED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_MIRROR, UNREADABLE or FAILED.
  #
  # NO_MIRROR is ordinary: a project that has never used the gate has nothing to measure against.
  outcome: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this project's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless 'measured'.
  behind: int,
  # Whether the number above means anything. False for a project with no upstream, and for one
  # this machine could not reach.
  measured: bool,
  # What the measurement itself reported: MEASURED, NEVER_CHECKED, NO_UPSTREAM, OFFLINE or FAILED.
  reason: string,
  detail: string
)

# What backups have been taken of a project's mirror.
#
# A bundle is written wherever an operator names it, so nothing could work this out afterwards -
# this is read from a record written when each one was taken. An interface cannot offer to restore
# from a list it cannot read, which is what this is for.
#
# Newest first. Empty is an ordinary answer: a project nobody has backed up, or one backed up by a
# Sokar older than the record.
method Backups(
  # Project NAME, as Projects reports it - not the path Prepare takes. A path answers an empty
  # list, and this list's empty case means "nothing is recorded for this project", which is
  # deliberately NOT the same sentence as "no bundle exists". So a wrong argument arrives as a
  # legitimate answer and somebody concludes their backups were never taken.
  project: string) -> (backups: []Backup)

type Backup (
  # When it was written, ISO-8601.
  taken: string,
  # Absolute path on the daemon's machine.
  bundle: string,
  # How many pushes were waiting for review when it was taken. What a restore would bring back.
  refs: int,
  # Whether that file is still there. A record is not the bundle: the file can be moved, deleted
  # or replaced afterwards and nothing here would know, so an absent one is shown rather than
  # quietly dropped - somebody moved it, and that is worth seeing.
  present: bool,
  # Its size now, or 0 when it is gone. Not what it was when taken: this is read from disk, and
  # claiming a size for a file that is not there would be inventing one.
  bytes: int
)

# Restores a project's mirror from a bundle.
#
# Refuses when the mirror holds pushes nobody has reviewed, and names them. Those exist ONLY in the
# mirror - not on the upstream, not in a workspace, not in the bundle being restored - so
# overwriting one destroys the only copy. Same outcome name as DeleteProject and Stop, for the same
# situation.
method RestoreBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects reports it - not the path Prepare takes. The bundle beside it is a
  # path; the two arguments of this method are named the same way and are not the same kind.
  project: string,
  # Absolute path of the bundle, as Backups reports it.
  bundle: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Proceeds although unreviewed work would be destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # RESTORED, PREVIEWED, HOLDS_WORK, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The mirror that would be written, or was.
  mirror: string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed. Non-empty with HOLDS_WORK, and also under force - it is what force
  # destroys, so it is worth showing afterwards as well as before.
  unreviewed: []string,
  detail: string
)

# Removes one backup: the bundle file and the record of it.
#
# The bundle may be somebody's only copy of work that never reached the gate, so this takes the
# path rather than an index - a list that shifted between reading it and acting on it would
# otherwise delete a different backup than the one somebody chose.
method DeleteBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects reports it - not the path Prepare takes.
  project: string,
  # Absolute path, exactly as Backups reported it.
  bundle: string,
  # Reports what would go, having removed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # DELETED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # Whether the file itself was there to remove. False with DELETED means the record was cleared
  # for a bundle somebody had already moved - which is a tidy-up, not a loss.
  fileRemoved: bool,
  # How many pushes it held, from the record. What a person is giving up.
  refs: int,
  detail: string
)

# Copies a credential an agent already holds on this machine into the vault.
#
# The one credential operation that carries no secret over this socket in either direction: the
# daemon reads a file on its own disk and writes its own vault, and all that crosses is an agent's
# name. So it is unaffected by whether storing a typed secret is ever allowed.
#
# It is the better first offer wherever an agent can log itself in, because nothing has to be
# retyped - and the commonest typo when retyping is storing the guide's placeholder.
method ImportCredential(
  # Which agent to import from. Omit when only one is installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Where it keeps its credentials. Omit for what the agent declares.
  configDirectory: ?string
) -> (
  # IMPORTED, NO_SUCH_AGENT, NO_CONFIG_DIRECTORY, NOTHING_TO_IMPORT, VAULT_LOCKED or FAILED.
  #
  # NOTHING_TO_IMPORT is the ordinary case rather than a fault: the agent is installed and nobody
  # has logged in with it here yet. VAULT_LOCKED is not a missing credential and must not be shown
  # as one - it is "we cannot tell you until you unlock it at the machine".
  outcome: string,
  # The vault key it was stored under. "" when nothing was stored.
  name: string,
  # The kind, as the agent recorded it. "" when nothing was stored.
  type: string,
  # How many characters arrived, so somebody can see that it worked without seeing what worked.
  # Never the value. 0 when nothing was stored.
  length: int,
  # The file it was read from, on the daemon's machine. "" when nothing was stored.
  source: string,
  # Why it failed, or a warning about what was stored - a credential that looks like a
  # placeholder, or one a task will refuse. "" when there is nothing to say.
  detail: string
)

# Which model providers this machine has, and which of them work could start against.
#
# The read half of authentication, and it carries no secret: names, kinds and whether something is
# stored, never a value.
#
# Cheap in the ordinary case. It consults the installed agents only when the vault holds a name
# that is not a provider's - the one situation where the key below can be an agent's name - so a
# vault written by a current Sokar costs nothing extra.
method Providers() -> (
  providers: []Provider,
  # Whether the vault could be read at all. False means locked or undecryptable, and then
  # 'authenticated' below is unknown rather than false - the same distinction Credentials draws,
  # for the same reason: "we cannot tell you until you unlock it" and "there is none" send a
  # person to different places.
  readable: bool
)

type Provider (
  # Short name, as an agent names it and as 'vault put' takes it.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable label.
  label: string,
  # Where requests are forwarded to.
  upstream: string,
  # Wire formats it serves, the provider's own first.
  dialects: []string,
  # Whether the vault holds a credential for it. MEANINGLESS unless 'readable' - render it only
  # beside that.
  authenticated: bool,
  # The kind stored: "api-key", "oauth", or "" when none is stored or none was recorded.
  credentialType: string,
  # The name a credential for this provider is stored under. Usually the provider's own name -
  # but a vault written before credentials were keyed by provider answers under the AGENT's name,
  # and that key stays in use so upgrading does not stop anyone authenticating.
  #
  # Computed here because a client cannot: intersecting "providers" with "stored names" reports a
  # missing credential for precisely the vault that has one, since the fallback key is invisible
  # from outside.
  credentialName: string,
  # The exact line to run at the machine to store one, ready to render verbatim. A subscription
  # token rather than an API key needs '--type oauth' on the end - they go in different headers,
  # and sending one as the other fails as an authentication error that looks like a wrong key.
  storeCommand: string
)

# Whether this machine can actually run a task.
#
# Every dependency below fails far from its cause - without nft a container comes up with no
# ruleset, without nsenter a clearance decision cannot reach a running task - so today a person
# learns about them by starting work and watching it behave strangely. This is the question they
# cannot otherwise ask: is this machine able to do the thing I am about to ask of it.
#
# It runs external programs to find out, so it takes a moment and is not something to poll. It is
# still a read: nothing here changes anything.
method Doctor() -> (
  probes: []Probe,
  # False when anything is MISSING. Not a summary a client should re-derive: this is the same rule
  # the CLI exits non-zero on, so the two cannot come to different conclusions about one machine.
  #
  # DEGRADED and UNKNOWN leave this true. The machine runs tasks, and the probes say how well.
  ready: bool
)

# One thing outside Sokar that a task depends on.
type Probe (
  # What is being reported, as an operator would name it.
  name: string,
  # OK, DEGRADED, MISSING or UNKNOWN. UNKNOWN is its own state rather than the good case: a probe
  # that guesses well is indistinguishable from one that works.
  state: string,
  # What was found, in one line.
  detail: string,
  # The single next action. "" only when the state is OK - a probe that fails and names nothing to
  # do about it cannot be constructed on this side, so an interface may render this without
  # checking whether it is there.
  action: string
)

method Node() -> (
  # Stable across restarts, and unique to this node. Random, and derived from nothing: it says
  # only whether two connections lead to the same place.
  #
  # A node is an OS user with a daemon, not a machine, so two accounts on one host answer
  # differently here - which is the case a hostname gets wrong.
  id: string
)

# Which logs a task has.
#
# Ask rather than assume: what a task writes depends on what it started - a task with no gate
# has no gate.log, one run with --clearance off has no clearance.log - so a client holding a
# list of names would show an empty viewer for a file that was never going to exist, and would
# never show one a later release adds. Empty for a task whose state directory is gone, which is
# what removal does.
method Logs(task: string) -> (logs: []Log)

# What a task holds that never reached the gate.
#
# Asked for ONE task, never on a listing. Every other field about a task comes from one 'ps' and
# the state directory; this one runs git inside the container, which on a list a client redraws
# would be a call per row - the cost 'since' and 'behindMeasured' both exist to avoid.
#
# 'readable' is not "holds nothing". Holding nothing is readable with two zeros. Unreadable means
# nobody could look: a task killed, or stopped by a Sokar that left no note. Absence rendering as
# nothing-to-worry-about is the failure this separation exists to prevent - the same one that put
# 'readable' on Credentials and Providers.
#
# 'asOf' is absent while the task runs, because the answer is current. For a stopped task it is
# when the answer was true: the workspace lives inside the container, so once it stops the only
# source is what the stop wrote down.
method WorkHeld(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  # Whether what it holds could be established at all.
  readable: bool,
  # Files changed and not committed.
  changedFiles: int,
  # Commits on no remote.
  unpushedCommits: int,
  # When this was true, ISO-8601. Absent while the task runs.
  asOf: ?string
)

method Tail(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # File name within the task's state directory, ending in .log. Not a path.
  log: string
) -> (lines: []string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the gate

# What is waiting at a project's gate.
#
# The gate methods take a project file rather than a task, because a gate belongs to a project
# and one daemon serves every project the operator has.
method Pending(
  # Path to the project file.
  project: string,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string
) -> (
  # Path to the bare mirror the agent pushes into.
  mirror: string,
  # offline, gatekeeping or online.
  mode: string,
  # Where the mirror was seeded from, or "" when it was not.
  seededFrom: string,
  pending: []PendingPush
)

# What a pending push contains, so it can be judged before it is forwarded.
method Review(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Ref name, from PendingPush.name.
  name: string,
  # What to diff against. Default: the upstream's default branch.
  against: ?string
) -> (
  # Unified diff.
  diff: string,
  # Commit log over the same range.
  log: string
)

# Forwards a pending push upstream. The only call in this interface that sends anything
# anywhere, and it makes the caller name the branch rather than inferring one.
method Approve(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  name: string,
  # Branch to push to. Required.
  branch: string
) -> (forwarded: string, branch: string)

# Drops a pending push. The work stays in the mirror; only the request is gone.
method Reject(project: string, upstream: ?string, name: string) -> (rejected: string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- clearance

# Blocked connections from every running task, as they happen.
#
# Streaming only: answering this once would look like it worked and then deliver nothing ever
# again, so a call without "more" is refused.
#
# One subscription covers every task, including tasks started after the call - an interface
# does not discover and connect to each task's own socket.
method Prompts() -> Prompt

# Answers one prompt.
#
# A task that is not running has no watcher to tell, and that is refused rather than accepted
# and dropped: an answer that goes nowhere leaves the operator believing they unblocked
# something.
method Decide(
  # Container name, from Prompt.task.
  task: string,
  # From Prompt.key, unchanged.
  key: string,
  # From Prompt.destination.
  address: string,
  # Whether to let it through.
  allow: ?bool
) -> (ok: bool)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- errors

# The name is not a task Sokar created.
#
# An error rather than an empty-looking answer, deliberately. WorkHeld answers "readable: false"
# for a task nobody could look inside, and a name that was never a task is not that - conflating
# them would let a client's wrong argument arrive as a legitimate answer, which is the failure
# the Backups documentation gap produced.
error NoSuchTask(task: string)

# The named log is not a file in that task's state directory.
error NoSuchLog(task: string, log: string)

# The method only works as a stream and was called without "more".
error StreamRequired(method: string)

# The task is not running, so nothing can act on the answer.
error NoClearance(task: string)

# A gate method was called without naming a project file.
# A rebuild depth this daemon does not know.
#
# Refused rather than treated as the default: quietly doing the cheapest thing when somebody asked
# for the most expensive is exactly the failure the depths exist to prevent.
error UnknownRebuild(rebuild: string)

error ProjectRequired()

# WidenTask was called without saying how far the change goes.
error ScopeRequired()

# Approve was called without a branch to push to.
error BranchRequired(name: string)

# Anything else that went wrong. The message is for a person, not for a client to match on.
error Failed(message: string)
